        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Salt does damage concrete, but it does so by making water and frost more destructive rather than by eating the slab outright. Mature, air-entrained, well-finished concrete handles moderate rock salt for decades. New concrete, weak surfaces and decorative finishes are where salt does its worst work.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The playbook is short: no deicers on new slabs, no ammonium products ever, a penetrating sealer every few years, and far less product than you are probably using now. Shovel first, scatter lightly, sweep up the leftovers, and fix the downspout that keeps making ice. Your driveway, your lawn and your car will all last longer.
          </p>
